# The first message binds a service to the session, once.
#
# `service` is a name. The page sends one, and the chassis resolves it
# against its own table — `browser` built in, `echo` added by the operator
# for this example — so the page can choose a service but can never choose
# a host or a port. An unknown name is refused with the names this node
# knows, which the responder below shows.
#
# `max_duration` bounds the whole binding — the lease expires, the
# connection is closed and the socket is told, whatever the client is doing
# — capped by --workspace-connect-max-duration.
#
# On success the chassis returns `_connect.connected = true` with the lease
# id and expiry; from here the socket is the service. A transport/provider
# failure (including "unavailable": nothing is listening — click /start
# first) merges as top-level `workspace.error`, which the responder renders.
WHEN .msg.type == "connect"
  EXEC "workspace://tools/connect"
    WITH service = .msg.service,
         max_duration = "1h",
         into = "_connect"
